{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — theme layout
  Phase 4, extended by Phase 8 and Phase 10. It carries the head, the header
  group, the content slot, the footer group, and — added in Phase 8 — the cart:
  its script, the drawer, the announcement region and the strings the script is
  allowed to say.

  Phase 10 made four changes here and nothing else:
    1. font_face is wrapped in {% style %}. Unwrapped it emitted raw CSS text
       into the head, which registered no font and rendered visibly on the page.
    2. The colour/type/layout :root block moved to snippets/css-variables.liquid,
       which Phase 1 section 29.2 specifies by name.
    3. base.css joins the stylesheet order as the global element layer.
    4. The footer group is rendered.

  An earlier version of this comment said the footer belonged to "Phase 16".
  That was wrong — the roadmap's Phase 16 is FINAL POLISH, and Phase 1 section
  29.1 places footer.liquid and footer-group.json in Phase 10's tree.

  Three Phase 1 findings are answered here because they live in the layout and
  because the header cannot be correct without them:

  HTML-02  The prototype's <html> had no lang attribute.
  SEO-01   document.title was empty.
  A11Y-02  There was no skip link and no <main> landmark, so a keyboard user had
           no way past the navigation.
{%- endcomment -%}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}" class="no-js">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible" content="IE=edge">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    {%- comment -%}
      Both of the colour scheme's head outputs come from one snippet so the
      scheme is resolved in exactly one place. This is the early half; the
      style half is emitted after the stylesheets below.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'css-variables', part: 'meta' %}
    <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">

    {%- comment -%}
      The favicon, at the three sizes that are actually asked for. Phase 16
      added the second and third: iOS ignores rel="icon" entirely and looks for
      apple-touch-icon at 180x180, and without it a customer who adds the store
      to their home screen gets a screenshot of the page instead of the mark.

      All three come from one Shopify setting, resized by Shopify, so there is
      one file to upload and no second asset to keep in step.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if settings.favicon != blank -%}
      <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 32, height: 32 }}">
      <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="192x192" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 192, height: 192 }}">
      <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="180x180" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 180, height: 180 }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    <title>
      {{ page_title }}
      {%- if current_tags %} &ndash; {{ 'general.tagged' | t }}: {{ current_tags | join: ', ' }}{% endif -%}
      {%- if current_page != 1 %} &ndash; {{ 'general.page' | t }} {{ current_page }}{% endif -%}
      {%- unless page_title contains shop.name %} &ndash; {{ shop.name }}{% endunless -%}
    </title>

    {%- if page_description -%}
      <meta name="description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Phase 16. Open Graph and Twitter card tags. Rendered from a snippet rather
      than written here because the image chain is four branches deep and the
      head should stay readable. Every value is a Shopify object; nothing in it
      is written copy. See snippets/meta-social.liquid.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'meta-social' %}

    {%- comment -%}
      Fonts. Phase 2 records the three approved families. Which of them Shopify
      can serve, and whether any must be self-hosted, is still open, so this
      loads only what the theme settings provide.

      font_face returns a bare @font-face RULE, not a style element — so these
      two calls have to sit inside one. Emitted unwrapped they did two things,
      both on every page: the faces were never registered, so Playfair Display
      and Jost never loaded and every heading and every line of body copy fell
      to a generic fallback; and because a non-whitespace character token ends
      the "in head" insertion mode, the CSS text itself rendered as visible
      content above the logo and everything after it in source order — the
      Shopify head, all five stylesheets, the :root block and both scripts —
      was parsed in body context.

      {% style %} is the form Dawn uses for exactly this filter.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      PHASE 16: THE WEIGHTS THE TYPE SYSTEM USES, NOT JUST THE ONES IT PICKS.

      These two calls emit a face for the ONE variant in each setting — jost_n4
      and playfair_display_n9. But design-tokens.css spends three weights on the
      body family: --type-eyebrow-weight is 500, and --type-label-weight and
      --type-price-weight are 600, between them covering every label, price and
      eyebrow in the theme. Neither had a face, so the browser synthesised them
      from Jost 400. Faux bold is thicker and wider than a real cut and tracks
      differently, which on a type system built out of tracked caps is visible
      on every surface.

      font_modify returns nil when the family has no such variant, so each is
      guarded: a merchant who picks a single-weight font gets exactly what they
      got before rather than a broken rule.

      Still inside one {% style %} — font_face returns a bare @font-face RULE,
      and the Phase 10 note below is the reason that matters.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign body_medium = settings.type_body_font | font_modify: 'weight', '500'
      assign body_semibold = settings.type_body_font | font_modify: 'weight', '600'
      assign display_italic = settings.type_display_font | font_modify: 'style', 'italic'
    -%}
    {% style %}
      {{ settings.type_display_font | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {{ settings.type_body_font | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {%- if body_medium %}
        {{ body_medium | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if body_semibold %}
        {{ body_semibold | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {%- endif %}
      {%- comment -%}
        THE DISPLAY ITALIC, added for the scripture pull-quotes.

        Until this line the theme had registered no italic and set no
        font-style anywhere — a grep for font-style across all 26 stylesheets
        returned nothing. A browser asked for italic with no italic face
        SYNTHESISES one by shearing the upright, which on a high-contrast
        display serif like Playfair smears the thick/thin relationship the
        face is chosen for. A quotation is exactly where that shows worst,
        because it is set large.

        This is not a third typeface: the two-typeface rule counts families,
        and an italic is a cut of the family already registered above.

        Guarded like the two weights above, and for the same reason —
        font_modify returns nil when the family has no such variant, so a
        merchant who swaps the display font for a single-cut face gets the
        upright rather than a broken @font-face rule.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if display_italic %}
        {{ display_italic | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {%- endif %}
    {% endstyle %}

    {{ content_for_header }}

    {%- comment -%}
      Order matters. design-tokens.css defines every custom property; base.css
      is the global element layer — reset, focus ring, motion suppression — and
      consumes those tokens, so it has to come second. Phase 10 split them: a
      file called design-tokens.css should define tokens and nothing else.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {{ 'design-tokens.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {{ 'base.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {{ 'header.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

    {%- comment -%}
      Shared components load from the layout; section stylesheets load from
      their own section. The button system is used by the hero and by every
      collection section, so it is a component rather than a section asset.
      Phase 5 section 16 recorded the promotion; Phase 6 performed it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Phase 18. The content column, which Phase 2 section 8 specified and the
      implementation never built — twelve private copies of four declarations
      until now. First of the components, because every other one sits inside
      it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {{ 'component-container.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {{ 'component-button.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

    {%- comment -%}
      Phase 8 promoted these two for the same reason Phase 6 promoted the
      button system: each has more than one consumer, and the cart drawer is
      rendered from this layout on nearly every page, so linking them from the
      sections as well put two <link> tags for the same URL on every product
      and cart page.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Phase 18 promoted the paginator for the reason Phase 6 promoted the button
      system: a second consumer appeared. It had been built twice, under two
      class systems, and the two had drifted.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {{ 'component-pagination.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {{ 'component-quantity.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {{ 'component-cart-line.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

    {%- comment -%}
      The only tokens a merchant may change, emitted after the stylesheets so
      these :root declarations win over the ones in design-tokens.css.

      Phase 10 moved the body of this block into snippets/css-variables.liquid,
      which Phase 1 section 29.2 specifies by name, and replaced the three free
      colour pickers with a verified scheme. The reasoning for the scheme is in
      the snippet: a Shopify color setting has no validation hook, so a merchant
      could silently invalidate every measured pairing in the system, and the
      light-surface gold partner had to travel with the gold it belongs to.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'css-variables', part: 'style' %}

    <script>document.documentElement.classList.replace('no-js', 'js');</script>
    <script src="{{ 'header.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
    {%- comment -%}
      Phase 8. The cart is reachable from every page — the header's control is
      on all of them, and so is the quick-add control a product grid may carry
      — so its behaviour is a layout script rather than a section one. Deferred,
      like the header's: nothing it enhances needs it in order to work.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <script src="{{ 'cart.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
  </head>

  <body class="template-{{ template.name | handle }}">
    <a class="skip-link" href="#MainContent">{{ 'accessibility.skip_to_content' | t }}</a>

    {% sections 'header-group' %}

    <main id="MainContent" tabindex="-1">
      {{ content_for_layout }}
    </main>

    {%- comment -%}
      Phase 8. The cart drawer is rendered here rather than inside a template,
      for three reasons:

      1. It has to exist on every page, because the header's cart control is on
         every page.
      2. Rendered from the layout it is a STATICALLY rendered section, so its
         section id is its filename — "cart-drawer" — which never changes. A
         section placed in a JSON template is given a generated id that does,
         and assets/cart.js would have to discover it at runtime.
      3. It must be a direct child of <body>. The script marks the page's other
         top-level regions inert while the drawer is open; a drawer nested
         inside one of them would be made inert along with it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Not on the cart page. There, the page IS the cart, and rendering the
      drawer as well put two views of one cart on one screen — which can
      disagree after an update, and which gave every cart line a duplicate DOM
      id, silently re-pointing the drawer's quantity labels at the page's
      inputs. On /cart the header's cart control stays what it is without
      scripting: a link to the page the customer is already on.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Phase 11 added the merchant's choice. With "Cart page" selected the drawer
      is not rendered at all — which is the whole switch, because assets/cart.js
      is already written to tolerate its absence: onOpenerClick returns early
      when Drawer.el is null, so the header control stays an ordinary link to
      the cart page, and the add-to-cart path gates auto-open on Drawer.el too.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if settings.cart_type != 'page' -%}
      {%- unless template.name == 'cart' -%}
        {% section 'cart-drawer' %}
      {%- endunless -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The cart's announcement region, and the strings the cart script is
      allowed to say.

      The region is in the layout, empty, rather than inside the drawer, for a
      specific reason: after every cart change the drawer's contents are
      replaced with freshly rendered markup, and a live region that is created
      at the same moment as its text announces nothing at all. It also carries
      data-cart-no-inert, because an inert subtree is removed from the
      accessibility tree and a live region inside one is silent.

      Every sentence comes from the locale file. The script writes no
      user-facing English of its own, so none of it can be untranslatable.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div
      id="CartStatus"
      class="visually-hidden"
      role="status"
      aria-live="polite"
      data-cart-no-inert
    ></div>

    <div
      hidden
      data-cart-no-inert
      data-cart-strings
      data-added="{{ 'cart.status.added' | t | escape }}"
      data-updated="{{ 'cart.status.updated' | t | escape }}"
      data-removed="{{ 'cart.status.removed' | t | escape }}"
      data-note-saved="{{ 'cart.status.note_saved' | t | escape }}"
      data-quantity-announce="{{ 'cart.status.quantity' | t | escape }}"
      data-error-generic="{{ 'cart.errors.generic' | t | escape }}"
      data-error-network="{{ 'cart.errors.network' | t | escape }}"
    ></div>

    {%- comment -%}
      The footer group, added in Phase 10. Phase 1 section 29.2 specifies both
      groups here: header-group above <main>, footer-group below it.

      It sits after the cart's live region rather than before it, because the
      region has to be reachable no matter what the footer contains, and the
      script marks the page's other top-level regions inert while the drawer is
      open — the footer among them.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% sections 'footer-group' %}
  </body>
</html>
